hume-miracles.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: hume-miracles.logic.r2 has "∀x (miracle(x) → violates_laws(x))" and "∀x (violates_laws(x) → uniform_against(x))"; hume-miracles.logic.r2.2 and hume-miracles.logic.r2.3 have "∀x (miracle(x) ↔ violates_laws(x))" and "∀x (violates_laws(x) → uniform_against(x))"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
hume-miracles.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps hume-miracles.logic.r2.2 and hume-miracles.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose nothing is true. Is S1, "A miracle is a violation of the laws of nature; and as a firm and unalterable experience has established these laws, the proof against a miracle, from the very nature of the fact, is as entire as any argument from experience can possibly be imagined.", true then?
hume-miracles.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: hume-miracles.logic.r2 has "∀x (uniform_against(x) → proof_against(x))" and "∀x (miracle(x) ∧ credible(x) → superior_opposite_proof(x))"; hume-miracles.logic.r2.2 and hume-miracles.logic.r2.3 have "∀x (uniform_against(x) → proof_against(x))" and "∀x (proof_against(x) ∧ credible(x) → superior_opposite_proof(x))"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
hume-miracles.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps hume-miracles.logic.r2.2 and hume-miracles.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose nothing is true. Is S7, "And as a uniform experience amounts to a proof, there is here a direct and full proof, from the nature of the fact, against the existence of any miracle; nor can such a proof be destroyed, or the miracle rendered credible, but by an opposite proof, which is superior.", true then?
hume-miracles.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: hume-miracles.logic.r2 has "∀x (miracle(x) → proof_against(x))"; hume-miracles.logic.r2.2 and hume-miracles.logic.r2.3 have "∀x (miracle(x) → proof_against(x) ∧ (credible(x) → superior_opposite_proof(x)))"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
